   "text": "Solar Generator for Camping: What Size and What It Runs \n Skip to content \n Esc \n ↑ ↓ to move · Enter to open · Esc to close\n Which size for which trip\n Trip Power station Panel \n Weekend: phones, lights, camera 300–500 Wh Optional, 60–100 W \n Weekend with a 12 V fridge About 1,000 Wh 100–200 W \n A week off-grid 1,000 Wh or more 200 W or more \n CPAP every night Depends on your machine; often 500–1,000 Wh 100–200 W \n How fast a panel refills it depends on the sun where you camp: roughly watts × peak sun hours × 75% a day. A 100 W panel gives about 440 Wh on an average Arizona day and about 300 Wh in Washington. Peak sun hours for every state are in our savings table .\n Tips that make a difference\n Aim the panel at the sun and move it a couple of times a day. That can make a big difference with a portable panel.\n Keep the power station in the shade; heat slows charging and wears the battery.\n Charge everything full before you leave, including from the car on the way.\n Use 12 V or USB outputs where you can; the AC outlets waste a little power converting it.\n It works at home too\n The same power station is a quiet backup during a power outage. How long it keeps a fridge running .\n Common questions\n What is a solar generator? Usually a portable power station sold together with one or more folding solar panels. It isn't a fuel generator: there's no engine, no noise and no fumes, so you can use it in a tent or van.\n What size solar generator do I need for camping? For phones, lights and a camera on a weekend, 300 to 500 Wh is plenty. Add a 12 V fridge or a CPAP machine and you'll want around 1,000 Wh plus a panel to recharge.\n Can I run a CPAP on a solar generator? Many people do. Check your machine's power rating and whether you use a heated humidifier, which uses much more. Multiply the watts by the hours you sleep to see what a night needs.\n Can I charge it from my car? Most power stations can charge from a car's 12 V socket, slowly, while you drive.\n More straight answers in the balcony solar FAQ .\n Sources\n Production and savings: our calculation method .\n On this page Which size Tips At home too Questions \n Keep reading\n RV and camper solar A power station and panels on the road. \n Backup power for outages How long a power station keeps you going. \n Best plug-in solar setup Kit, power station or battery system? \n Is it worth it? When balcony solar pays, and when it doesn't.",
